              "segment": {
                "start_index": 447,
                "end_index": 672,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Trigonometry (and the Table of Chords)\nHipparchus is widely considered the **\"father of trigonometry.\"** To calculate the eccentric and epicyclic orbits of the Sun and the Moon, he needed a way to solve mathematical triangles"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 675,
                "end_index": 785,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Invention:** He created the first known **Table of Chords** (contained in a lost twelve-book treatise)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 787,
                "end_index": 871,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "A \"chord\" represents the length of a straight line connecting two points on a circle"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 874,
                "end_index": 970,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Why it matters:** This table was the direct mathematical precursor to our modern sine tables"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 972,
                "end_index": 1069,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It allowed astronomers to systematically solve any plane or spherical triangle for the first time"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1079,
                "end_index": 1289,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Planispheric Astrolabe\nWhile the invention of the astrolabe is sometimes debated, many historians of science credit Hipparchus as the creator (or at least the co-developer) of the **planispheric astrolabe**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1291,
                "end_index": 1362,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Invention:** The astrolabe is a hand-held model of the universe"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1364,
                "end_index": 1495,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It utilized stereographic projection (which Hipparchus developed) to project the 3D celestial sphere onto a flat, 2D portable plate"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1497,
                "end_index": 1655,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Why it matters:** It allowed users to calculate the precise positions of the Sun and stars, determine local time (by day or night), and calculate latitude"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1764,
                "end_index": 1904,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Sighting Instruments (The Dioptra)\nTo measure the heavens with unprecedented accuracy, Hipparchus had to design his own physical instruments"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1906,
                "end_index": 2042,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Invention:** He invented (or significantly improved) the **dioptra** (or diopter), a sighting tube or rod with a moveable target"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2045,
                "end_index": 2156,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Why it matters:** He used this instrument to measure the apparent angular diameters of the Sun and the Moon"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2158,
                "end_index": 2312,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "He also likely designed or refined early versions of the **armillary sphere**\u2014a model of objects in the sky consisting of a spherical framework of rings"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2322,
                "end_index": 2530,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Stellar Magnitude Scale\nHipparchus invented a system to classify the brightness of stars, which he did while compiling the Western world's first comprehensive star catalog (which mapped roughly 850 stars)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2532,
                "end_index": 2607,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Invention:** He divided stars into **six magnitudes of brightness**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2609,
                "end_index": 2739,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The brightest stars were designated as \"first magnitude,\" while the faintest stars visible to the naked eye were \"sixth magnitude\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2971,
                "end_index": 3114,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "A Systematic Latitude and Longitude Grid\nWhile the concepts of latitude and longitude existed before him, Hipparchus revolutionized cartography"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3116,
                "end_index": 3277,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Invention:** He was the first to suggest that the geography of the Earth should be mapped on a systematic **mathematical grid of latitude and longitude**"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3279,
                "end_index": 3549,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Why it matters:** He proposed that local coordinates should be determined strictly by astronomical observations\u2014such as measuring the height of the stars or timing solar eclipses from different parts of the world\u2014rather than relying on estimated travel distances"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3557,
                "end_index": 3852,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### His Most Famous *Discoveries* (Distinct from Inventions):\nThough not physical inventions, Hipparchus is also famous for discovering:\n* **The Precession of the Equinoxes:** He realized that the positions of the stars shift very slowly over centuries due to a wobble in Earth's rotational axis"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3854,
                "end_index": 4113,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Distance to the Moon:** Using geometry and observations of eclipses, he calculated the distance from the Earth to the Moon with astonishing accuracy (estimating it to be roughly 60 Earth radii, which is incredibly close to the actual average of ~60.3)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
